   claimed dead-loop-variable removal (`for cents, idxs := range byCents {
   _ = cents ...}` -> `for _, idxs := range byCents`). transfers_test.go
   diffs byte-identical (no output). Claim holds.

2. Map-iteration nondeterminism (edge-set built from `range byCents`).
   Built a 40-row fixture landing in one $20.00 bucket (pattern-hit legs
   across 5 accounts/20 dates) and ran `Classify` 200 times in one process,
   fingerprinting every row's TransferPairKey plus the full suspected-queue
   pair-key/reason set each run. Identical on all 200 runs
   (TestProbe_ManyEqualBucketDeterminism, go test -count=1, PASS). The
   `sort.Slice` tiebreak on `PairKeyFor(...)` (content, not slice position)
   does its job.

3. Layer-boundary / degree-counting angles.
   - Asymmetric degree: Y has two dist-1 candidates X and Z (degree 2); X
     and Z each have only Y (degree 1 for themselves). Result: neither
     X, Y, nor Z is paired; both edges (X,Y) and (Y,Z) are queued
     `ambiguous`, per code's own degree map applying to both endpoints of
     an edge, not just the locally->1 one. Matches doc ("every edge
     touching it goes to review").
   - Stale edge after pairing: A-B pair at distance 1 (layer 1); C is a
     distance-3 candidate for both. A and B pair correctly; C is left
     unpaired (its only real candidates were claimed), not resurrected,
     no double TransferPairKey write. The `paired[e.i]||paired[e.j]`
     staleness filter at each layer's start works.
   - Could not construct a case where a row is both `ambiguous` and
     `TransferClass==paired`: `pairLegs` is only called for edges that
     fail the `degree>1` test in the *same* degree map used to set
     `ambiguous`, so the two are mutually exclusive by construction in
     pass 2, verified by code trace and by the probes above.

4. "Tie semantic changed" (pre-fix auto-paired first index on an exact
   tie; new code queues both). Checked the package's top-of-file doc
   comment and GLOSSARY.md ("Internal transfer"): both say "an exact tie
   goes to review rather than guessing" / equivalent language, and that
   text is UNCHANGED by this diff (`git diff HEAD` shows only the pass-2
   body and its inline comment changed). The pre-fix behaviour was
   actually a violation of that documented rule (order accident, not a
   real tie-break), so the new behaviour is the one consistent with the
   doc. No contradiction found.

5. Regression surface. `git diff HEAD -- transfers.go` confirms only the
   pass-2 body/comment changed; `applyDecisions`, the rejected-pair
   exclusion, pass 3 (suggestions), pass 4 (external typing), the
   `Suspected` sort, and `Paired`/`External` counting are byte-identical
