6. Test-quality check (does not eyeball test names). Copied HEAD's
   pre-fix transfers.go into a scratch package alongside the NEW test
   file and ran the three new order-independence tests:
   `TestClassify_ClosestDatePairingIsOrderIndependent` and
   `TestClassify_ABCCounterexamplePairsClosestInEveryOrder` both FAIL
   against the old implementation (permutations where B is visited before
   A wrongly pair A-B instead of the closer A-C, exactly the described
   defect); `TestClassify_PermutationsNeverDropOrDuplicateRows` passes on
   both (correctly scoped — it isn't testing the order bug). All three,
   plus the rest of the suite (14 pre-existing tests, unmodified),
   PASS against the merged code. Not vacuous.

7. Complexity (non-blocking observation, not a SPEC criterion).
   Benchmarked a single pattern-hit, same-magnitude bucket at n=200/1000/
   3000 rows: 5ms / 107ms / 1.1s, consistent with the pairwise edge
   enumeration being O(bucket^2). This requires hundreds-to-thousands of
   rows sharing one exact cent amount AND a transfer-pattern hit
   (InternalTransferPatterns is a narrow substring list: "schwab
   moneylink", "usaa funds transfer", etc.), which is unlikely in a
   single-user ledger. SPEC's S2 acceptance criteria (order independence,
   closest-date, no-drop, Paired/External as row counts, unchanged pass
   3) say nothing about performance, so this is reported as a finding,
   not a failing criterion.

8. One pre-existing, non-regression observation: an `ambiguous` row from
   pass 2 is not excluded from pass 3's suggestion scan (pass 3 only
   checks `paired[i]`), so it can pick up an additional suspected entry
   for a farther candidate beyond its already-queued nearest tie
   (reproduced with TestProbe_AmbiguousRowReenteringPass3). Confirmed via
   `git diff HEAD` that pass 3's code is byte-identical to HEAD -- this
   behaviour predates S2 and is not something this diff introduced or
   was tasked to fix ("pass 3 (suggestions) untouched" is itself the
   acceptance criterion, and it is in fact untouched). Not a basis for
